Random notes about using cvc5 to work on graph conditions

cvc5 doesn't consider empty universes, like most FOL solvers. Hence a condition
like "There exists a node but no edges" is unsatisfiable (when using Node and
Edge sorts) and "there are no nodes" is unsatisfiable.

As a workaround, there are two options:

- For Node+Edge sorts, if a condition is detected as UNSAT, run a second check,
this time excluding all edge predicates entirely (setting them to false) and
without the Edge sort. If it is still UNSAT, then the only possible model that
cvc5 cannot find would be the empty graph, which can be tested separately.

- Switch to one-sorted logic, in which case only the empty graph remains as a
special case.


Settings that affect formula generation:
- Injective or non-injective matching
- Edges allowed (only necessary for many-sorted logic, see above)


******************

Using CVC5 as a solver together with other solvers

It doesn't really run in parallel with others and moving it to a separate thread
seems kind of scary (how to interrupt it?). Running it in a subprocess seems
better. This raises the question how to get the condition into the subprocess
and the model out of the subprocess.

The important things are actually the origin of the condition, because a model
has to start at it. Others can be recreated as needed. Idea: to each object we
assign an unique identifier (possibly incorporating identityHashCode, but note
that it doesn't *have to* be unique) then we serialize using these names.
(Consider using SGFWriter! Extend it so it can reveal names of written objects!)

To retain the mapping, we map all elements to unique names, then generate a bunch
of partial morphisms for them, to be pushed into SgfWriter together with the
condition. After model finding, we serialize the model and these partial morphisms
again, which, on the other side, gives us the correct mappings again!

Most of this stuff *should* in theory be possible by just tracking the names that
are assigned during writing SGF, but that'd require opening a few bits of the API
a bit, so that should be done carefully.